{"text": " That is it for stores. Also check out the lecture after this lecture where I also prepared some additional things you can do with stores. But the main thing about stores is that you can create writable and readable stores. Readable stores are great for pieces of data that update automatically. Writable stores will be the main stores you interact with because they allow you to both subscribe to them and trigger changes in the data manually based on user events and so on. And you also learned about custom stores which is a useful enhancement for writable stores where you can hide away some of the complexity from your components and put it into the store file instead and have all your heavy lifting, all your logic in the store file. And in the components you can just call easy to use methods like remove item and all the magic happens behind the scenes. Stores are super useful features in Svelte because they allow you to outsource logic and most importantly data management into dedicated files. They make sure that you don't have infinitely long prop and custom event chains and they particularly make communicating between loosely related components like the product and the card component here much easier. Stores are super useful. Stores are super useful.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 1.6999999999999997, "end": 9.2, "text": " That is it for stores. Also check out the lecture after this lecture where I also prepared some additional things you can do with stores.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 307, 309, 337, 9512, 13, 2743, 1520, 484, 264, 7991, 934, 341, 7991, 689, 286, 611, 4927, 512, 4497, 721, 291, 393, 360, 365, 9512, 13, 50865], "temperature": 0, "avg_logprob": -0.1369403058832342, "compression_ratio": 1.798206278026906, "no_speech_prob": 4.2580820813764575e-11, "words": [{"word": " That", "start": 1.6999999999999997, "end": 2.34, "probability": 0.75341796875}, {"word": " is", "start": 2.34, "end": 2.5, "probability": 0.9853515625}, {"word": " it",